    @section ncursestestingcapture Composed Frame Capture

    @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session::capture() "Session::capture()" returns typed
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::ScreenCapture "ScreenCapture" data for the completed physical
    screen, including overlapping panels. Its cells are in row-major order:
    \c frame.cells[y*frame.cols+x]. A leading wide cell contains text and its width;
    each continuation contains empty text, width zero and \c continuation=True.
    Combining marks remain on their leading cell. Each cell records attributes,
    the session-local color pair, and foreground/background palette indices (-1
    for terminal defaults; NOTHING when colors are disabled).

    Finish drawing first: call Application::drawAll(), or call Session::updatePanels()
    then Session::doupdate(). Leave base touched lines intact for panel composition.
    Capture does not flush pending work. Window::refresh() and Panel::update() also
    complete output. Inspect focus and scrolling on the UI thread at that same
    checkpoint. Repeated captures do not move live cursors, consume input, change
    panel order, invoke callbacks or change subsequent drawing.

    The cursor uses zero-based physical screen coordinates, including when hidden.
    Visibility records the last supported request: 0 hidden, 1 normal, 2 very visible,
    or -1 unknown/unsupported. A failed request preserves the previously known state.
    Normal application focus preserves the session cursor setting; modal focus manages
    its own visibility. Choose \c cursor=1 for an ordinary editor session.

    The hash is directly serializable with \c make_json() from the json module and
    has schema version 1. Capture records ncurses' emitted-screen model, not pixels
    read back from an emulator. Pin terminal type and locale for comparisons. Fonts,
    RGB palette customization, blink phase, unsupported rendition, out-of-band output,
    and terminal-specific Unicode rendering need real-terminal/browser review.
    Combining sequences are bounded by ncurses' cchar_t capacity. Attribute masks
    are ncurses ABI values; \c supported_attrs reports terminal capabilities. ACS
    cells retain their alternate-character-set attribute.

    \c snapshot() retains its older base-window text behavior; it is not composed
    capture and should not be used for overlay approval. Run the complete example:
    @verbatim
QORE_MODULE_DIR=build-debug:qlib qore --enable-debug --warnings-are-errors examples/composed-capture.qr
    @endverbatim

    For deterministic resize, change TestTerminal::resize(rows, cols), then call
    Session::resize(rows, cols), deliver an InputType::Resize event to the application,
    and draw before capture. Session::resize() changes ncurses buffers; it does not
    resize the device or run application layout. Session::terminalSize() reports the
    device dimensions independently of this logical viewport. No signal, delay or polling is required.

    @section ncursestestingterminal Headless Drawing and Input Tests

    @ref Qore::Ncurses::TestTerminal "TestTerminal" creates a pseudo-terminal with fixed dimensions.
    Pass it through the \c test_terminal option to @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session "Session".
    Tests can then draw, inspect text with @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::readText() "Window::readText()",
    or capture rows with @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session::snapshot() "Session::snapshot()".

    @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session::injectEvent() "Session::injectEvent()" queues a typed event;
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session::injectEvents() "Session::injectEvents()" queues a sequence.
    Each event is delivered once, with mouse coordinates and button state preserved. A batch
    is read in list order; separate injections push input ahead of already pending events.
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session::flushInput() "Session::flushInput()" discards pending input.
    The module queue holds 4,096 input entries; a Paste event occupies one entry and
    queued paste payloads share a 64 MiB bound. Injection failure rolls back the entire
    call, preserving previously queued events. Consume pending input before retrying.
    Paste payloads use the session byte limit (1 MiB by default); inject them with
    InputType::Paste and the complete text in the \c ch field. Alt events accept one
    Unicode character or Enter. TestTerminal::writeInput() handles partial PTY writes;
    large input should be produced concurrently with the reader.
    Injection avoids keyboard timing and does not need a human-operated terminal. This complete
    test covers drawing, character input, mouse input, and a timeout:

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires ncurses

Ncurses::TestTerminal terminal(24, 80);
on_exit terminal.close();
Ncurses::Session session({"test_terminal": terminal});
on_exit session.close();
Ncurses::Window window = session.screen();
window.drawText(0, 0, "Ready");
window.refresh();
@assert(window.readText(0, 0, 5) == "Ready");
@assert(session.snapshot(0, 1, 5)[0] == "Ready");

session.injectEvent(<Ncurses::InputEvent>{"type": Ncurses::InputType::Char, "ch": "x"});
*hash<Ncurses::InputEvent> event = window.readEvent(100, 25);
@assert(event && event.type == Ncurses::InputType::Char && event.ch == "x");
session.injectEvent(<Ncurses::InputEvent>{
    "type": Ncurses::InputType::Mouse,
    "mouse": <Ncurses::MouseEvent>{"x": 4, "y": 2, "button_state": Ncurses::BUTTON1_CLICKED},
});
event = window.readEvent(100, 25);
@assert(event && event.type == Ncurses::InputType::Mouse && event.mouse.x == 4);
@assert(!window.readEvent(25, 25));
    @endcode

    @section ncursestestingwidgets Testing Widgets Without an Event Loop

    Create @ref NcursesUi::Application "Application" with the test session, then call
    @ref NcursesUi::Application::processEvent() "Application::processEvent()" and
    @ref NcursesUi::Application::drawAll() "Application::drawAll()" directly. This lets assertions
    run synchronously. The following test verifies checkbox input and state:

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires ncurses
%requires NcursesUi

Ncurses::TestTerminal terminal(24, 80);
on_exit terminal.close();
Ncurses::Session session({"test_terminal": terminal});
on_exit session.close();
NcursesUi::Application app(session);
NcursesUi::CheckboxWidget checkbox("Enabled", False);
app.setRoot(checkbox);
checkbox.setFocused(True);
@assert(!checkbox.isChecked());
hash<NcursesUi::EventResult> result = app.processEvent(<Ncurses::InputEvent>{
    "type": Ncurses::InputType::Char, "ch": " ",
});
@assert(result.handled);
@assert(checkbox.isChecked());
app.drawAll();
    @endcode

    The source checkout also provides \c test/TestHarness.qc, a test-only wrapper for the terminal
    and session with input/assertion helpers. It is not an installed class in
    @ref ncursesuiintro "NcursesUi"; include that source explicitly when using the repository's
    test harness. See @ref ncurses_mouseguide "Mouse Support" for event fields and
    @ref ncurses_ui_widgetsguide "UI Widget Toolkit" for application lifecycle rules.
    @section ncursestestingbrowser Browser Interaction and Visual Review

    The checkout's \c review directory runs the same native stories through a PTY
    and xterm.js inside Storybook. Node 24 and Python 3.9+ are tooling prerequisites;
    the native gallery remains independently usable. From the repository root:
    @verbatim
cmake --build build-debug -j4
cd review
npm ci
npx playwright install chromium
npm run build
npm test
npm run review
    @endverbatim
    Use <tt>npm run dev</tt> for interactive review at http://127.0.0.1:6006.
    The bridge accepts only registered IDs, binds locally, and uses native draw,
    PTY sequence, xterm parse and render completion signals. It does not synchronize
    captures with sleeps or polling. Tests replay default and mouse scenarios
    through actual terminal bytes and compare native cells, cursor, focus and scroll.

    The visual matrix includes all applicable states, color/monochrome and viewport
    presets. \c review/artifacts/index.html links baseline/current/diff images and
    semantic changes. Missing or changed evidence fails; CI never accepts baselines.
    See [review/README.md](https://github.com/qoretechnologies/module-ncurses/blob/develop/review/README.md)
    for explicit reviewed acceptance, adding cases, font/terminal capability limits,
    browser-reserved shortcuts, prerequisites and CI artifacts. Interaction review
    observations accompany automated results; they are not a human usability study.
*/
